enchilada: sweetidle=1, idle CPUs power their core down (PSCI CPU_SUSPEND)

Idle CPUs only ran WFI. With sweetidle=1 (=2: rail power-down 0x40000004) an idle cpu1-7 whose next timer is at least sweetidle_us (2000) away calls PSCI CPU_SUSPEND 0x40000003 (sdm845.dtsi "power-down", local-timer-stop) with sweet_secondary_entry as the entry point, and comes back the way XNU's own non-retention idle does: reset_vector -> resume_idle_cpu -> arm_init_idle_cpu, which now redoes the GIC CPU interface (PE_init_cpu) and the CNTV deadline (ClearIdlePop). The lost per-core timer is covered as Linux does it, by the memory-mapped timer (timer@17c90000 frame 0, CNTP, GIC SPI 7) routed to cpu0, which never powers down and IPIs each sleeper 1 ms before its deadline. At boot SweetCPU::startAll checks PSCI_FEATURES, CNTACR and CNTFRQ and self-tests the broadcast IRQ; any failure keeps WFI. One line every 10 s: power-downs per CPU, share of time powered down, early returns, PSCI errors. Without sweetidle nothing changes.

